1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,012
A drive shaft. A riser
joint. A subsea connector.

2
00:00:03,062 --> 00:00:06,324
In service, steel is rarely
loaded one way at a time:

3
00:00:06,374 --> 00:00:09,074
it is pulled, bent and twisted, all at once.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:14,136
Inside, the loads build up a stress field.
Zoom in to a single material point, and

5
00:00:14,186 --> 00:00:18,805
on the faces of an infinitesimal cube,
three normal stresses pull or push, while

6
00:00:18,855 --> 00:00:21,956
three shear stresses slide
the faces past one another.

7
00:00:22,006 --> 00:00:26,392
Together they form the Cauchy stress
tensor: symmetric, with six independent

8
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:27,034
components.

9
00:00:27,333 --> 00:00:31,593
But the strength we measure comes from a
uniaxial tension test, as a single number:

10
00:00:31,643 --> 00:00:35,694
the yield strength. So how do we decide
whether a full three-dimensional stress

11
00:00:35,744 --> 00:00:37,511
state will make the material yield?

12
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:41,484
Checking each component against the
yield strength fails immediately.

13
00:00:41,534 --> 00:00:45,975
A shaft in pure torsion yields when its
shear stress reaches only about fifty-eight

14
00:00:46,025 --> 00:00:50,358
percent of the tensile yield strength.
And a solid specimen squeezed equally from

15
00:00:50,408 --> 00:00:53,551
all sides, far beyond that
strength, does not yield at all.

16
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:58,024
We need one equivalent stress, built from
the whole tensor, comparable with the

17
00:00:58,074 --> 00:01:02,264
tension test. To find it, we must first
understand what yielding physically is.

18
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:14,557
Structural steel is a polycrystal: an
aggregate of grains, each a nearly perfect

19
00:01:14,607 --> 00:01:17,501
lattice of iron atoms,
each oriented differently.

20
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:20,932
Inside a grain, plastic
deformation happens by slip:

21
00:01:20,982 --> 00:01:26,073
planes of atoms shearing past one another
along close-packed directions, leaving the

22
00:01:26,123 --> 00:01:28,521
lattice intact and the volume unchanged.

23
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:33,828
Sliding a whole plane at once would break
every bond across it simultaneously,

24
00:01:33,878 --> 00:01:37,408
needing roughly the shear
modulus over 2π: gigapascals.

25
00:01:37,458 --> 00:01:40,272
Real metals yield orders of magnitude lower.

26
00:01:40,567 --> 00:01:45,462
The reason is the dislocation: a line
defect, here an extra half-plane of atoms.

27
00:01:45,512 --> 00:01:48,862
Under shear it glides,
re-forming bonds₁ row at a time.

28
00:01:48,912 --> 00:01:52,633
When it exits, the crystal has
slipped by one atomic spacing.

29
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:57,200
What drives it is the shear stress
resolved onto the slip plane, in the slip

30
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:02,007
direction: Schmid's law. Even a crystal in
pure tension yields by shear, on inclined

31
00:02:02,057 --> 00:02:02,408
planes.

32
00:02:02,733 --> 00:02:07,483
Now press equally from all sides. The
traction on every plane is purely normal:

33
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:12,282
no plane carries shear. No resolved shear,
no glide, no yielding, as Bridgman's

34
00:02:12,332 --> 00:02:14,469
high-pressure experiments confirmed.

35
00:02:14,767 --> 00:02:20,148
So we split the stress tensor in two. The
mean stress, σ_m, one third of the trace,

36
00:02:20,198 --> 00:02:23,485
defines a hydrostatic part:
σ_m times the identity.

37
00:02:23,535 --> 00:02:27,280
What remains is the deviatoric
stress, s, with zero trace.

38
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:33,177
In an isotropic material, hydrostatic
stress makes a cube grow or shrink, but it

39
00:02:33,227 --> 00:02:35,911
stays a cube: volume
changes, shape does not.

40
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:39,135
Under pure shear, it
distorts at constant volume:

41
00:02:39,185 --> 00:02:41,024
shape changes, volume does not.

42
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:45,662
Under combined loading, it does both, and
only the distortion is linked to slip.

43
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:50,392
That is why the criterion we want is based
on distortion energy, rather than the

44
00:02:50,442 --> 00:02:55,127
total strain energy that Beltrami proposed.
Total energy includes volume change, and

45
00:02:55,177 --> 00:02:57,550
would predict yielding under pure pressure.

46
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:05,520
Start with the principal stresses. In one
particular orientation, every shear stress

47
00:03:05,570 --> 00:03:10,991
vanishes. The normal stresses that remain,
σ₁, σ₂ and σ₃, are the eigenvalues of the

48
00:03:11,041 --> 00:03:14,182
tensor: the roots of its
characteristic equation.

49
00:03:14,467 --> 00:03:19,983
Its coefficients, I₁, I₂ and I₃, are
invariants, independent of our choice of axes.

50
00:03:20,033 --> 00:03:24,677
For an isotropic material, a yield
criterion can depend only on these.

51
00:03:24,967 --> 00:03:30,283
The mean stress is₁ third of I₁. Subtracting
it gives the principal deviators, s₁,

52
00:03:30,333 --> 00:03:35,780
s₂ and s₃, which always sum to zero, and
do not change when all three stresses shift

53
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:36,369
together.

54
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:41,190
Now the energy: one half of
stress, double-dotted with strain.

55
00:03:41,240 --> 00:03:45,946
In Hooke's law, the mean stress drives
volume change through the bulk modulus, K,

56
00:03:45,996 --> 00:03:49,703
and the deviator drives distortion
through the shear modulus, G.

57
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,557
Multiply out, and the cross terms vanish,
because the identity double-dotted with s

58
00:03:55,607 --> 00:04:01,163
is the trace of s: zero. The energy separates
exactly, into a volumetric part and a

59
00:04:01,213 --> 00:04:03,595
distortional part: s:s, over four G.

60
00:04:03,867 --> 00:04:08,333
Half of s:s is the second
deviatoric invariant, J₂.

61
00:04:08,383 --> 00:04:11,345
So the distortion energy is J₂/2G.

62
00:04:11,633 --> 00:04:16,277
Now the key step. Because the deviators sum
to zero, the sum of their squares can be

63
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:20,916
written using only their differences. And
a difference of deviators is a difference

64
00:04:20,966 --> 00:04:25,498
of principal stresses: the mean cancels.
So J₂ is₁ sixth of the sum of the squared

65
00:04:25,548 --> 00:04:27,118
principal stress differences.

66
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:32,760
Huber, von Mises and Hencky proposed that
yielding begins when the distortion energy

67
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:37,720
reaches its value at yield in a tension
test: σ₁ equal to the yield strength,

68
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:42,679
written σ_Y to distinguish it from the
component σ_y, and the other two zero.

69
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:46,225
Equate the two energies.
The shear modulus cancels:

70
00:04:46,275 --> 00:04:49,649
the criterion does not depend
on the elastic constants.

71
00:04:49,699 --> 00:04:52,948
What remains defines the
von Mises equivalent stress:

72
00:04:52,998 --> 00:04:57,928
the square root of one half the sum of
the squared principal stress differences.

73
00:04:57,978 --> 00:04:59,235
Equivalently, √(3J₂).

74
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:04,353
There is a physical reading, too. Average
the squared shear stress over every plane

75
00:05:04,403 --> 00:05:08,720
through the point: the root-mean-square
shear stress is proportional to √J₂.

76
00:05:08,770 --> 00:05:13,144
For a polycrystal, with slip systems in
every direction, the von Mises stress

77
00:05:13,194 --> 00:05:15,615
measures the shear available to drive slip.

78
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:22,237
Solvers report Cartesian components. In x,
y and z, each shear stress appears twice

79
00:05:22,287 --> 00:05:28,547
in s:s, since τ_xy equals τ_yx. So it
contributes its full square to J₂, and three

80
00:05:28,597 --> 00:05:30,548
times its square to σ_vm².

81
00:05:30,833 --> 00:05:35,170
The normal stresses enter as squares
minus cross products, which depend only on

82
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:36,169
their differences.

83
00:05:36,467 --> 00:05:39,445
Yielding begins when σ_vm
reaches σ_Y. Precisely:

84
00:05:39,495 --> 00:05:44,203
the onset of yield at a point, in an
isotropic, ductile, pressure-insensitive

85
00:05:44,253 --> 00:05:47,540
material, with equal yield
in tension and compression.

86
00:05:47,590 --> 00:05:52,299
It says nothing about ultimate failure.
Necking and fracture come much later,

87
00:05:52,349 --> 00:05:54,153
governed by different physics.

88
00:05:56,633 --> 00:06:00,233
Let's test the criterion
on five loading cases.

89
00:06:00,283 --> 00:06:04,815
Case A, uniaxial tension: σ₁
equals σ, the others are zero.

90
00:06:04,865 --> 00:06:09,863
Two differences equal σ, one is
zero, and σ_vm equals σ, exactly.

91
00:06:10,133 --> 00:06:14,119
By construction: the criterion
was calibrated on this test.

92
00:06:14,169 --> 00:06:17,813
Yet even here, a hydrostatic
part, σ/3, plays no role.

93
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:27,307
Case B, pure shear: only τ_xy is non-zero.
The Cartesian form leaves₃ τ squared, so

94
00:06:27,357 --> 00:06:32,281
σ_vm is √3 τ, and yield
in shear occurs at σ_Y/√3:

95
00:06:32,331 --> 00:06:37,155
0.577 σ_Y. For S355 steel,
about 205 megapascals.

96
00:06:37,433 --> 00:06:40,767
Why √3? Rotate the element
by forty-five degrees:

97
00:06:40,817 --> 00:06:45,186
pure shear is a tension τ and a
compression −τ, at right angles.

98
00:06:45,236 --> 00:06:50,848
Its principal differences are τ, τ and two
τ: a sum of squares of 6τ², against 2σ²

99
00:06:50,898 --> 00:06:56,440
for tension. So shear reaches the critical
distortion at a stress √3 times lower.

100
00:06:56,733 --> 00:07:01,298
Case C, equal biaxial tension,
as in a thin spherical shell.

101
00:07:01,348 --> 00:07:05,605
The differences are zero, σ
and σ: again, σ_vm equals σ.

102
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:11,366
Why? Equal biaxial tension is a hydrostatic
tension σ, plus a uniaxial compression,

103
00:07:11,416 --> 00:07:16,883
−σ, in the third direction. The hydrostatic
part is invisible, so despite two equal

104
00:07:16,933 --> 00:07:21,269
stresses, the state carries a full
uniaxial measure of distortion.

105
00:07:21,567 --> 00:07:25,869
Case D, hydrostatic stress: all
three principal stresses equal p.

106
00:07:25,919 --> 00:07:29,886
Every difference vanishes, so
σ_vm is zero, at any pressure.

107
00:07:30,167 --> 00:07:34,438
A solid steel part at three thousand
metres carries thirty megapascals of

108
00:07:34,488 --> 00:07:37,458
hydrostatic compression,
and zero von Mises stress.

109
00:07:37,508 --> 00:07:42,194
A sealed pipe beside it, with one atmosphere
inside, carries a large compressive

110
00:07:42,244 --> 00:07:47,167
hoop stress, and can yield or collapse. It
is the pressure difference that distorts.

111
00:07:47,467 --> 00:07:52,491
Here the classical theory has limits:
soils, concrete, polymers and porous metals

112
00:07:52,541 --> 00:07:57,002
are pressure-sensitive, and need
criteria like Drucker–Prager or Gurson.

113
00:07:57,052 --> 00:08:02,014
And in steel, hydrostatic tension causes
no yield, yet it drives void growth and

114
00:08:02,064 --> 00:08:03,142
reduces ductility.

115
00:08:03,433 --> 00:08:08,915
Case E: a shaft under axial force and
torque. At the surface, the axial force gives

116
00:08:08,965 --> 00:08:12,314
a normal stress σ, and the
torque a shear stress τ.

117
00:08:12,364 --> 00:08:16,446
The von Mises stress is the
square root of σ² plus₃ τ squared.

118
00:08:16,767 --> 00:08:20,814
In the σ–τ plane, that is an
ellipse through σ_Y and σ_Y/√3.

119
00:08:20,864 --> 00:08:26,346
In 1931, Taylor and Quinney tested
thin-walled tubes in exactly this combination;

120
00:08:26,396 --> 00:08:32,015
their yield points lay closer to this
ellipse than to the maximum shear prediction.

121
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:40,686
Now see it all at once. With the principal
stresses as coordinates, every stress

122
00:08:40,736 --> 00:08:45,149
state is a point. The line where all
three are equal is the hydrostatic axis.

123
00:08:45,199 --> 00:08:49,785
Perpendicular to it lie the deviatoric
planes; the one through the origin is the

124
00:08:49,835 --> 00:08:50,249
π-plane.

125
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:55,873
A stress point splits into a component along
the axis, set by the mean stress, and a

126
00:08:55,923 --> 00:09:01,039
component in the deviatoric plane: the
deviator itself, at a distance √(2J₂) from

127
00:09:01,089 --> 00:09:01,613
the axis.

128
00:09:01,933 --> 00:09:05,904
The von Mises condition fixes
J₂, so it fixes that distance:

129
00:09:05,954 --> 00:09:10,193
√(2/3) σ_Y, everywhere along the
axis and all the way around it.

130
00:09:10,243 --> 00:09:12,940
The yield surface is a circular cylinder.

131
00:09:13,267 --> 00:09:17,792
Circular, because the criterion ignores
the Lode angle around the axis, which

132
00:09:17,842 --> 00:09:22,725
carries the third invariant. Infinitely
long, because moving along the axis is pure

133
00:09:22,775 --> 00:09:23,854
hydrostatic stress.

134
00:09:24,133 --> 00:09:28,829
Slice it with the plane σ₃ equals zero,
and out comes the plane-stress yield

135
00:09:28,879 --> 00:09:33,949
ellipse, with uniaxial tension, pure shear
and equal biaxial tension exactly where

136
00:09:33,999 --> 00:09:35,010
we computed them.

137
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:40,408
Now increase the loads proportionally.
The point moves out along a ray, and meets

138
00:09:40,458 --> 00:09:43,593
the cylinder when the load
factor equals σ_Y/σ_vm:

139
00:09:43,643 --> 00:09:48,369
the safety factor against first yield.
A hydrostatic path never reaches it.

140
00:09:50,867 --> 00:09:54,375
The other classical criterion
is Tresca's, from 1864:

141
00:09:54,425 --> 00:09:59,745
yielding begins when the maximum shear
stress, half the largest principal stress

142
00:09:59,795 --> 00:10:05,250
difference, reaches σ_Y/2. Tresca pictures
slip on the single most stressed plane;

143
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:08,003
von Mises averages shear over all planes.

144
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:11,456
Its surface is a hexagonal
prism around the same axis.

145
00:10:11,506 --> 00:10:15,315
In the π-plane, the hexagon is
inscribed in the von Mises circle:

146
00:10:15,365 --> 00:10:20,125
they agree at the six vertices, the uniaxial
and equal-biaxial states, and differ

147
00:10:20,175 --> 00:10:23,509
most at the midpoints of the
sides, which are pure shear.

148
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:27,324
So Tresca is always equal
or more conservative.

149
00:10:27,374 --> 00:10:32,874
In pure shear, it predicts yield at
0.5 σ_Y, against 0.577 for von Mises:

150
00:10:32,924 --> 00:10:38,501
about fifteen percent apart. Most
ductile metals fall closer to von Mises.

151
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:43,610
And von Mises is smooth: its normal,
the direction of plastic flow, is unique

152
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:48,281
everywhere. That enables the robust
radial return algorithm used for metal

153
00:10:48,331 --> 00:10:51,563
plasticity in essentially
every finite element code.

154
00:10:51,613 --> 00:10:54,908
At Tresca's corners, the
flow direction is ambiguous.

155
00:10:57,467 --> 00:11:02,496
Now a real analysis: a forged steel transition
for a subsea tubular, where the wall

156
00:11:02,546 --> 00:11:07,453
steps up to a thick hub through a shoulder
fillet, under axial force, bending and

157
00:11:07,503 --> 00:11:07,882
torque.

158
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:12,486
It is meshed with quadratic tetrahedra,
refined toward the fillet, where the stress

159
00:11:12,536 --> 00:11:13,531
gradients are steep.

160
00:11:13,833 --> 00:11:18,526
The solver finds the nodal displacements.
In each element, it differentiates them at

161
00:11:18,576 --> 00:11:23,156
the integration points to get strain, and
Hooke's law gives the full stress tensor

162
00:11:23,206 --> 00:11:27,730
at every Gauss point; then principal
stresses from its eigenvalues, and von Mises

163
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:29,254
stress from its invariants.

164
00:11:29,533 --> 00:11:33,357
For plotting, results are extrapolated
to the nodes and averaged.

165
00:11:33,407 --> 00:11:38,125
Averaging components, then computing von
Mises, differs from averaging von Mises

166
00:11:38,175 --> 00:11:42,952
values; and large jumps between unaveraged
values flag a mesh that is too coarse.

167
00:11:43,233 --> 00:11:45,974
Here is the raw tensor in
cylindrical coordinates:

168
00:11:46,024 --> 00:11:50,495
axial stress from tension and bending,
shear from the torque, and hoop and radial

169
00:11:50,545 --> 00:11:55,015
stresses only at the step. None of them
alone tells you whether the steel yields.

170
00:11:55,300 --> 00:11:59,815
Combined point by point, they give
one field: the von Mises stress.

171
00:11:59,865 --> 00:12:05,266
About 147 megapascals in the pipe wall,
rising to 300 at the root of the fillet.

172
00:12:05,567 --> 00:12:08,359
Why the peak? Picture the
load flowing through the wall:

173
00:12:08,409 --> 00:12:12,622
at the shoulder, the flow lines turn into
the hub, and crowd together at the fillet.

174
00:12:12,672 --> 00:12:14,855
The tighter the radius, the higher the peak.

175
00:12:15,129 --> 00:12:19,135
At a real fillet, refining the
mesh makes the peak converge.

176
00:12:19,185 --> 00:12:24,206
Model the corner as perfectly sharp,
and elasticity predicts a singularity:

177
00:12:24,256 --> 00:12:29,682
stress rising like r^−0.46. Each refinement
reports a higher peak, without limit:

178
00:12:29,732 --> 00:12:33,332
a number set by your element
size, not by your design.

179
00:12:33,629 --> 00:12:38,250
So never take the maximum at face value.
Check convergence, and model real radii.

180
00:12:38,300 --> 00:12:42,749
Where codes require it, linearize the
stresses through the wall into membrane,

181
00:12:42,799 --> 00:12:46,382
bending and peak components, and
apply the right limit to each.

182
00:12:46,662 --> 00:12:51,531
And remember the material model. A linear
elastic analysis reports stresses above

183
00:12:51,581 --> 00:12:55,539
yield, while real steel yields
locally and redistributes the load.

184
00:12:55,589 --> 00:13:00,518
A local elastic peak above σ_Y calls for
an elastic-plastic or fatigue assessment;

185
00:13:00,568 --> 00:13:02,157
it is not proof of failure.

186
00:13:04,696 --> 00:13:09,293
Let's finish with numbers. The
yield strength is 355 megapascals.

187
00:13:09,343 --> 00:13:15,228
At the critical point, the principal stresses
are 240, 80 and minus 40 megapascals.

188
00:13:15,496 --> 00:13:20,578
The mean stress is 280 over
three: 93.3 megapascals.

189
00:13:20,628 --> 00:13:28,769
Subtracting it gives the deviators: 146.7,
minus 13.3 and minus 133.3, which sum to

190
00:13:28,819 --> 00:13:29,263
zero.

191
00:13:29,562 --> 00:13:36,312
The principal differences
are 160, 120 and minus 280.

192
00:13:36,362 --> 00:13:43,625
Their squares, 25,600, 14,400
and 78,400, sum to 118,400.

193
00:13:43,675 --> 00:13:46,576
Half of that is 59,200.

194
00:13:46,862 --> 00:13:53,342
The square root gives the von Mises stress:
243.3 megapascals, confirmed by three J₂

195
00:13:53,392 --> 00:13:54,818
from the deviators.

196
00:13:55,096 --> 00:13:59,450
The safety factor against
first yield is 355 over 243.3:

197
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:03,617
1.46, a utilization of 69
percent. Tresca gives 1.27:

198
00:14:03,667 --> 00:14:09,122
more conservative. The largest
principal stress alone would give 1.48:

199
00:14:09,172 --> 00:14:14,863
slightly unconservative, because it
ignores the compressive third stress.

200
00:14:15,162 --> 00:14:18,777
Keep the terms distinct. Yield
strength is a material property.

201
00:14:18,827 --> 00:14:22,034
Equivalent stress describes
the stress state at a point.

202
00:14:22,084 --> 00:14:26,804
The safety factor is their ratio. And the
design allowable is what a code permits:

203
00:14:26,854 --> 00:14:31,108
the yield strength reduced by partial
or usage factors for uncertainty and

204
00:14:31,158 --> 00:14:31,806
consequence.

205
00:14:32,096 --> 00:14:35,123
And staying below yield is
necessary, not sufficient.

206
00:14:35,173 --> 00:14:38,490
Welded joints fail in fatigue
at stresses far below yield.

207
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:43,134
Slender members and shells buckle. Cracks
cause fracture, governed by toughness.

208
00:14:43,184 --> 00:14:47,778
And excessive deformation can make a
connector leak long before anything yields.

209
00:14:48,062 --> 00:14:52,515
So here is the central insight. A
material does not yield because one stress

210
00:14:52,565 --> 00:14:57,314
component becomes large. Every stress
state has a hydrostatic part, which changes

211
00:14:57,364 --> 00:15:00,336
volume, and a deviatoric
part, which changes shape.

212
00:15:00,386 --> 00:15:04,779
In ductile metals, distortion drives
slip, and distortion governs yielding.

213
00:15:05,096 --> 00:15:09,330
The von Mises stress measures exactly that:
the square root of one half the sum of

214
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:11,472
the squared principal stress differences.

215
00:15:11,762 --> 00:15:15,999
Von Mises stress transforms a complex
three-dimensional stress state into one

216
00:15:16,049 --> 00:15:20,620
physically meaningful quantity that can be
compared against the material's uniaxial

217
00:15:20,670 --> 00:15:21,455
yield strength.

